# What must never enter a build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second matters more than the size:
#
#   1. speed — node_modules and build output are re-created inside the image,
#      so shipping them wastes minutes on every build;
#   2. safety — .env holds the database password, the Auth0 secret and the IMAP
#      password, and storage/pdf holds real customer transport orders. Neither
#      belongs in an image that gets pushed to a registry.
#
# Anything a build genuinely needs is listed as an exception at the end.

# Dependencies and build output: rebuilt inside the image.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
out
**/out
coverage
**/coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets. Every variant, so a stray .env.production cannot slip through.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Real customer documents. The application writes these into a volume at
# runtime; they must never be baked into an image.
storage/pdf

# Version control and editor state.
.git
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea
*.swp

# Local tooling noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
.pnpm-store

# Docker's own files: changing a compose file must not invalidate an image layer.
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile*
**/Dockerfile*
.dockerignore

# Documentation and scratch material. The PDF fixtures under docs/06-pdf are the
# one exception: the backend image runs a parser smoke test against them at
# build time, and they are committed test documents rather than customer data.
docs/00-project
docs/01-architecture
docs/02-business
docs/03-ui
docs/04-development
docs/05-pricing
README
